      "title": "How Stanford Teaches AI Coding Now",
      "summary": "What does it take to build AI agents that deliver useful, reliable work? Stanford lecturer and Monaco Head of AI Mihail Eric joins Demetrios for a conversation inside a Waymo about how AI is changing software development - and what it takes to put agents into production. Mihail explains why he rebuilt most of his Modern Software Developer course, how he teaches students to avoid shipping AI slop, and why research, planning, and verification still matter as coding agents become more capable. They discuss Claude Code, agent loops, context engineering, memory and compaction, and how to design MCP tools around what an agent needs to accomplish. Then they go inside Monaco\u2019s approach to sales agents, from turning meetings and emails into useful insights to building a custom agent harness. Along the way: lessons from Boris and Lee Robinson\u2019s guest lectures, why company traces are becoming valuable assets, and Monaco\u2019s unusual approach to brand building. What\u2019s the biggest bottleneck in your agent workflows: context, tools, or verification? Let us know in the comments. Timestamps: 00:00 Meet Mihail Eric 00:16 Teaching AI Coding at Stanford 02:42 Avoiding AI Slop 04:48 Research, Plan, Build, Test 06:19 Verifying Agent Work 09:16 Lessons from Claude Code\u2019s Creator 12:40 Proactive AI Assistants 16:13 Agent Memory & Context 17:41 Rethinking Git for Agents 19:32 AI Startup Pitches 22:51 Why Traces Matter 24:09 Better MCP Tools 26:01 Monaco\u2019s Sales Agents 29:40 Turning Conversations into Insights 33:33 AI Meets Human Expertise 35:03 Poker & Brand Building 36:46 Inside Monaco\u2019s Agent Stack 38:44 Designing Tools Around Intent 40:12 Building an AI Advantage 42:53 Wrapping Up",
